# Is one E11 code enough to call someone diabetic?
prompt: "How do you filter on ICD-10 and ATC without losing subgroups — str.startswith, isin or regex? What happens to the cohort when you require two contacts instead of one, or add ATC A10 from lmr? What do you win and lose with a strict cohort definition?"
next: {"title":"From events to patients: how do you collapse millions of rows into one row per person?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/registerdata-i-praksis-python-og-pandas/fra-hendelser-til-pasienter-hvordan.cast"}
enroll: https://drawcast.anvil.app

## Is one E11 code enough? — One code, one patient?
lang: en
voice: female
level: basic

    text tittel "Is one E11 code enough?" x 500 y 700 font_size 40 hidden true
    ```python q x 500 y 390 width 920 font_size 17 show left hidden true
    import pandas as pd
    npr = pd.DataFrame({
        "pid": [4711] * 3,
        "dato_inn": ["2019-03-12", "2021-06-02",
                     "2022-01-20"],
        "icd10": ["S52.5", "E11.9", "J18.9"]})
    npr[npr["icd10"].str.startswith("E11")]
    ```
    box n_code "One E11.9 code" #8a5fa8 x 200 y 420 hidden true
    box n_true "Real type 2 diabetes" #2f6b8f x 720 y 600 hidden true
    box n_wrong "Miscoded: really E10" #b5482e x 720 y 420 hidden true
    box n_susp "Work-up, no diagnosis" #b5482e x 720 y 240 hidden true
    arrow a_true n_code -> n_true hidden true
    arrow a_wrong n_code -> n_wrong hidden true
    arrow a_susp n_code -> n_susp hidden true
    box n_missed "GP only: no E11 in npr" #8f887c x 230 y 290 hidden true
    text l_missed "The opposite error" #8f887c x 230 y 345 font_size 26 hidden true
    box f1 "Code filter: E11" #8a5fa8 x 170 y 500 hidden true
    mark mark_f1 target f1 kind box hidden true
    box f2 "kohort: one row per pid" x 500 y 500 hidden true
    box f3 "Prevalence" x 820 y 620 hidden true
    box f4 "Drug use" x 820 y 500 hidden true
    box f5 "Risk estimates" x 820 y 360 hidden true
    arrow e1 f1 -> f2 hidden true
    arrow e2 f2 -> f3 hidden true
    arrow e3 f2 -> f4 hidden true
    arrow e4 f2 -> f5 hidden true

This series builds a type 2 diabetes cohort from register data — and the whole result hangs on one choice you make before any analysis: how much does it take to call someone diabetic?
    draw tittel

Here are all the npr rows for one patient, pid 4711: three contacts between 2019 and 2022.
    draw q q_line_1 q_line_2 q_line_3 q_line_4 q_line_5 q_line_6 parallel true

We pick out the contacts where icd10 starts with E11 — the code for type 2 diabetes.
    draw q_line_7

One row comes back: a contact on 2 June 2021, coded E11.9.
    draw q_out

So is pid 4711 diabetic — or is this just one code in a file?
    highlight q_out effect glow

    pause 0.6

    clear keep ["tittel"]

That single code can hide at least three quite different realities.
    draw n_code

The most common is the simplest: the patient has type 2 diabetes and got the code in hospital.
    draw a_true n_true

But ICD-10 in npr is as much a language of funding as a clinical one — the code follows the DRG payment, and a type 1 patient can end up on E11.
    draw a_wrong n_wrong

Or the code was set during a work-up that ended without a diabetes diagnosis.
    draw a_susp n_susp

    pause 0.4

And then there's the opposite error, the one that does the most harm: most type 2 diabetes is followed up by GPs, and those patients never set foot in npr.
    draw n_missed l_missed

So one code hit catches the people who have been to hospital — imprecise, but cheap — and is completely blind to everyone who never went.
    focus n_true n_missed

    pause 0.5

    clear keep ["tittel"]

The choice looks like one line of code in a filter.
    draw f1 mark_f1

But it decides who gets a row at all in the kohort table we build through the course.
    draw e1 f2

And the cohort sets the prevalence, the drug use and every single risk estimate you report afterwards.
    draw e2 f3 e3 f4 e4 f5

Say a wide filter gives sixty thousand patients and a strict one forty-five thousand: the same raw data, fifteen thousand people apart — and neither number is wrong.
    highlight f2 effect glow

So: is one E11 code enough? It isn't a fact about the patient but a hypothesis — and the rest of this lecture tests it with more contacts and with A10 from lmr.
    text konklusjon "Defining the cohort is an analytic choice, not a detail." x 500 y 235 font_size 30
    mark mark_k target konklusjon kind box
    delivery grave

## startswith, isin or regex?
lang: en
voice: female
level: basic
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - isin(['E11'])
      - startswith('E11')
      - match(r'^E11')
    values: '{filt.tall}'
    value_labels: true
    y_label: Rows matched (of 8)
    title: Catching E11 – without losing subcodes

    ```python filt show code hidden true
    import pandas as pd
    koder = ["E11", "E11.9", "E116", "E110", None, "E10.9", "E11.6", "I10"]
    npr = pd.DataFrame({"pid": range(8), "icd10": koder})
    eksakt = npr["icd10"].isin(["E11"]).sum()
    kode = npr["icd10"].fillna("").str.replace(".", "", regex=False)
    prefiks = kode.str.startswith("E11").sum()
    regexp = kode.str.match(r"^E11").sum()
    tall = [int(eksakt), int(prefiks), int(regexp)]
    lmr = pd.DataFrame({"atc": ["A10BA02", "A10A", "A10BK01", "A11CC", None]})
    a10 = int(lmr["atc"].fillna("").str.startswith("A10").sum())
    ```

Now that we've seen how one E11 code makes a fragile cohort, here's a simpler question that's at least as dangerous: a filter that looks right can delete patients without telling you.
    draw title filt filt_line_1 parallel true

Eight contacts from npr — look at the diagnosis column: E11, E11.9, E116, E110, an empty cell, E10.9, E11.6 and I10.
    draw filt_line_2 filt_line_3

E11.6 is type 2 diabetes with other specified complications — just as diabetic as E11, only coded more precisely, and written with or without the dot depending on who delivered the file.
    point at.ref filt_line_2 gesture underline

    pause 0.3

The first attempt is isin with a list: exact matches, and only exact matches.
    draw filt_line_4

One row out of eight.
    draw axes bar_1

Four of the other seven rows aren't errors in the data — they're patients we just lost, without a single error message.
    highlight bar_1 effect glow delivery grave

    pause 0.4

So we normalise first: fill empty cells with an empty string and strip the dot — without fillna, indexing with the mask stops with Cannot mask with non-boolean array containing NA / NaN values.
    draw filt_line_5

startswith on the normalised column catches five rows — and leaves E109 behind, because type 1 diabetes doesn't belong in this cohort.
    draw filt_line_6 bar_2

Regex gives exactly the same answer, because str.match is anchored at the start — but with str.contains you must write the anchor, ^E11, yourself, or E11 also matches in the middle of a longer string.
    draw filt_line_7 filt_line_8 bar_3

    pause 0.3

Same trick in lmr: A10 catches metformin, A10BA02, plus A10A and A10BK01, but not A11CC — ATC is strictly hierarchical, so there the prefix IS the subgroup; in ICD-10 a prefix only works once the format is cleaned.
    draw filt_line_9 filt_line_10

So what do you win and lose with each of the three?
    clear keep ["title","axes","bar_1","bar_2","bar_3"]

startswith is fast and copes with codes that grow extra characters — but it takes the code exactly as written, dots and all, and needs na=False or a fillna in front.
    text t1 "startswith: fast, needs na=False" x 245 y 560 font_size 24

isin is the most auditable: you write down exactly which codes count — and that's exactly why it loses the subcodes you forgot.
    text t2 "isin: auditable, loses E11.6" x 245 y 480 font_size 24

Regex can do everything the other two can, which makes it the easiest to get subtly wrong without anything crashing.
    text t3 "regex: precise, mind the ^" x 245 y 400 font_size 24

Now you can see where the cohort is really decided: normalise the column first and match on the prefix, and it's your definition — not the string format of the delivery — that decides who counts as diabetic.
    text t4 "Normalise first, match the prefix" #b5482e x 245 y 295 font_size 25
    mark mark_t4 target t4 kind box
    delivery grave

## Strict or wide: what do you win and lose?
lang: en
voice: female
level: basic
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - ≥1 E11
      - ≥2 E11
      - ≥2 E11 or A10
    values:
      - 12400
      - 8900
      - 11600
    value_labels: true
    y_label: Patients
    title: Three definitions, same data

    box node_vid "Wide: ≥1 E11" #b5482e x 265 y 600 hidden true
    box node_streng "Strict: ≥2 or A10" #2f6b8f x 735 y 600 hidden true
    text t_vid_p "+ Catches nearly all the sick" #b5482e x 265 y 500 font_size 27 hidden true
    text t_vid_m1 "− Working diagnoses get in" #b5482e x 265 y 445 font_size 27 hidden true
    text t_vid_m2 "− Noise pulls effects to zero" #b5482e x 265 y 390 font_size 27 hidden true
    text t_str_p "+ Nearly all truly sick" #2f6b8f x 735 y 500 font_size 27 hidden true
    text t_str_m1 "− Loses the mildest" #2f6b8f x 735 y 445 font_size 27 hidden true
    text t_str_m2 "− Skewed to sicker patients" #2f6b8f x 735 y 390 font_size 27 hidden true
    text t_middle "The definition must fit the question — not the reverse." x 500 y 260 font_size 30 hidden true
    mark mark_middle target t_middle kind box hidden true
    ```python def_kode x 500 y 380 width 960 font_size 17 show left hidden true
    import pandas as pd
    npr = pd.DataFrame({"pid": [1, 1, 2, 3],
        "icd10": ["E11.9", "E119", "E10.9", "E11"]})
    lmr = pd.DataFrame({"pid": [2, 3],
        "atc": ["A10BA02"] * 2})
    kode = npr.icd10.str.replace(
        ".", "", regex=False)
    e11 = npr.loc[kode.str.startswith("E11"), "pid"]
    teller = e11.value_counts()
    fra_npr = set(teller[teller >= 2].index)
    fra_lmr = set(lmr.loc[
        lmr.atc.str.startswith("A10"), "pid"])
    kohort = sorted(fra_npr | fra_lmr)
    print(kohort)
    ```

Now that we've seen what two contacts and an A10 prescription do to the cohort, the choice remains — and it decides who your prevalence figure is about.
    draw title axes

With one E11 code in npr we have 12,400 adults — the widest definition we can make.
    draw bar_1

If we require two contacts on different dates, we're left with 8,900. Almost one person in three has gone.
    draw bar_2

If we also accept everyone with an A10 dispensing in lmr, we're back up to 11,600.
    draw bar_3

    pause 0.4

But are the 3,500 who dropped out miscodings we got rid of — or patients we lost?
    point at.ref bar_2 gesture circle

Both, and the two errors do different damage.
    clear

The wide definition catches almost everyone who really has type 2 diabetes.
    draw node_vid t_vid_p

But suspected cases and working diagnoses come along, and non-differential misclassification usually pulls the effect estimate towards zero — you understate, you don't overstate.
    draw t_vid_m1 t_vid_m2

    pause 0.3

The strict definition requires two contacts or a drug, and then nearly everyone in the cohort is truly ill.
    draw node_streng t_str_p

The price is the mildest cases — the ones followed by their GP who never end up in npr — so your cohort is sicker than Norway is.
    draw t_str_m1 t_str_m2

If you're estimating prevalence, being strict costs you; if you're measuring the effect of metformin, being wide costs you.
    draw t_middle mark_middle delivery grave

What saves the analysis isn't choosing right, but writing the choice down as code others can run.
    clear

Here's the whole cohort definition, with a tiny toy npr and lmr so it actually runs.
    draw def_kode def_kode_line_1 def_kode_line_2 def_kode_line_3 def_kode_line_4 def_kode_line_5 parallel true

First we strip the dot from the code and pick everything that starts with E11, so both E11.9 and E119 are included.
    draw def_kode_line_6 def_kode_line_7 def_kode_line_8

Then we count contacts per pid and require at least two — and take everyone with A10 from lmr.
    draw def_kode_line_9 def_kode_line_10 def_kode_line_11 def_kode_line_12

The union gives the cohort: three patients, in for three different reasons.
    draw def_kode_line_13 def_kode_line_14 def_kode_out

So: is one E11 code enough? To find everyone, yes; to be sure, no — what's enough is a definition you can justify, count the consequences of, and run again.
    highlight def_kode_out effect glow delivery grave

